      "bot": false,
      "body": "⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —\n⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the watchdog poll timer busy-spins ~1 core for the entire lifetime of every attempt**\n\nThe new watchdog uses `read -t 1 -u 3` with fd 3 opened from `/dev/zero` as its 1-second poll timer (`scripts/load_drop_retry.sh:170` + `:181`, reused at `:195`/`:199` for the drain loop). `read` on `/dev/zero` never blocks and never yields a newline, so the builtin loops on instant `read()` syscalls for the full second — this is a hot poll loop, not a timer.\n\nMeasured on this checkout (the real script, stubbed runner):\n\n- 30 s wall run with a sleeping stub: **30.25 CPU-seconds consumed** (8.20 user + 22.05 sys) ≈ one full core pinned for the whole run.\n- Sampled at 3 s elapsed: the wrapper bash at **99.3% CPU** (`ps -o pcpu`), while the stub itself slept at ~0%.\n\nWhy it matters: this watchdog now runs on every attempt of every wrapped leg — the PTY integration shards (ci.yml:1470), the core unit shards (ci.yml:1011), the jsr trio (ci.yml:1047), and the local fast gate (ci_fast_gate.sh:252). A healthy shard run pays one pinned core for its full duration (a 5-min shard ≈ 5 core-minutes stolen), and the PTY legs are precisely the load-sensitive class (FA_PTY_BUDGET_SCALE, \"SM dispatch waves pack the hosted pool\") whose flakes this PR heals — the fix adds sustained CPU contention to the conditions that induce the failure.\n\nThe intent behind `/dev/zero` (\"killing this subshell mid-wait forks no `sleep` child for the job harness to report\") can be kept with a true zero-CPU, zero-child timer: open a watchdog-private FIFO `O_RDWR` — `exec 3<>\"${CONSOLE}.wdg.fifo\"` — an O_RDWR fifo never EOFs (the process's own write end holds it), so `read -t 1 -u 3` blocks silently until the timeout. Verified in this sandbox: 3 × `read -t 1` polls = 3009 ms elapsed, **0.00 CPU** (`/usr/bin/time`: 0% of wall). Add the fifo to the existing `cleanup` trap. (Do NOT point fd 3 at the console fifo itself — reading it would steal the runner's output from `tee`.)\n\nAlternatively `sleep 1` would also work but forks the child you deliberately avoided, so the fifo timer is the closer match to the stated constraint. The ≤1 s drain loop at `:195` is bounded and can keep the `/dev/zero` form or take the same fix."
    },
